  "form": "Research architecture",
  "pages": 21,
  "question": "How can AI-assisted work on an open problem persist as a record that people and models can check, understand and extend?",
  "precis": "A correct Lean theorem can be misrepresented when its hypotheses disappear from a paper. This account follows a weighted-support criterion for Erdős Problem 257 through its mathematical argument, two registered formal supports and written explanation. It describes source inspection, publication checks, editorial revision and contributions in a repository covering eight problems. A historical test rejected nine false edits but missed a false completion claim; the paper distinguishes that record from the limited follow-up check. Source-byte agreement and repository adoption do not establish mathematical meaning, independent review or recognition by the community. The paper retains its mathematical antecedents and compares the recorded relations with blueprints and Prove2Me, without claiming comparative benefit.",
  "owns": "the research architecture: problem-centred collections, the path from a proof to a public claim, and the contribution cycle",
  "not_authority_for": "the mathematics it uses as examples, which the problem papers own, or the novelty and significance of those results",
